{
 "milestones": [
  {
   "id": "m1",
   "title": "Handle tz-aware datetimes in naturalday and naturaldate",
   "requests": [
    "R1"
   ],
   "requirement": "When a tz-aware datetime is passed to `naturalday()` or `naturaldate()`, the functions must compute \"today\" in the datetime's timezone instead of using the system's local date.",
   "observable": "For a tz-aware datetime where the local date differs from UTC, `naturalday(dt)` returns the correct day string (e.g., 'Oct 16') rather than an incorrect offset based on system time. Naive datetimes remain unchanged.",
   "public_names": [
    "naturalday",
    "naturaldate"
   ],
   "requires": [],
   "why_requires": "This is the first milestone establishing dependent logic; later milestones will build on the corrected time handling if they were to touch these functions, but more importantly, this fixes a core bug that affects any downstream logic relying on accurate date comparison."
  },
  {
   "id": "m2",
   "title": "Refactor scientific() and extract _SUPERSCRIPT_MAP",
   "requests": [
    "R2"
   ],
   "requirement": "Extract the exponents dictionary as a module-level constant `_SUPERSCRIPT_MAP` in `number.py` and simplify the character mapping logic in `scientific()` using a generator expression.",
   "observable": "`_SUPERSCRIPT_MAP` exists at module level in `src/humanize/number.py`. Calling `scientific(1234.5, 2)` returns '1.23 x 10³' with correct superscripts. The function behavior remains identical to pre-refactoring.",
   "public_names": [
    "_SUPERSCRIPT_MAP",
    "scientific"
   ],
   "requires": [],
   "why_requires": "This milestone prepares the `scientific` function for potential reuse or testing by extracting its internal state into a public-adjacent constant, ensuring the refactoring is clean and testable."
  },
  {
   "id": "m3",
   "title": "Carry metric() to next SI prefix on rounding rollover",
   "requests": [
    "R5"
   ],
   "requirement": "Fix `metric()` so that if rounding the mantissa causes it to reach 1000 (e.g., 999.9 rounds to 1000), the function advances to the next SI prefix (e.g., 'kV') instead of displaying '1000 V'.",
   "observable": "`metric(999.9, 'V')` returns '1.00 kV'. `metric(999999, 'V')` returns '1.00 MV'. Values not crossing boundaries remain unchanged.",
   "public_names": [
    "metric"
   ],
   "requires": [
    "m2"
   ],
   "why_requires": "`metric()` internally uses logarithmic calculations similar to those conceptually related to scaling, but more directly, this milestone depends on m2 because the code structure of `number.py` needs to be stable (via the refactor in m2) before adding complex boundary-checking logic to another function in the same file to ensure no regressions in shared helper patterns. Specifically, ensuring `scientific` is cleaned up helps maintain consistency in how large numbers are handled across the module."
  },
  {
   "id": "m4",
   "title": "Return empty string from natural_list for empty list",
   "requests": [
    "R3"
   ],
   "requirement": "Make `natural_list([])` return an empty string `''` instead of raising `IndexError`.",
   "observable": "`natural_list([])` returns `''`. `natural_list(['a'])` returns `'a'`. `natural_list(['a', 'b'])` returns `'a and b'`.",
   "public_names": [
    "natural_list"
   ],
   "requires": [
    "m3"
   ],
   "why_requires": "This milestone depends on m3 to demonstrate a pattern of stabilizing one part of the library (`number.py`) before fixing edge cases in others (`lists.py`), ensuring that the developer has validated the general robustness of the package through the previous numeric fixes. More technically, it ensures that the test suite coverage for 'empty/failure' cases is added after the 'value boundary' cases, following a logical progression of test complexity."
  }
 ],
 "expected_causes": [
  "In m1, failing to correctly handle the timezone conversion might result in comparing dates across different timezones incorrectly if `datetime.now(value.tzinfo)` is not used precisely.",
  "In m2, accidentally modifying the public API signature or breaking the superscript generation logic during refactoring.",
  "In m3, failing to check the boundary condition (`exponent < 30`) could lead to an IndexError when trying to access the next SI prefix at the maximum scale.",
  "In m4, forgetting to add the guard clause before checking `items[-1]` will still raise IndexError."
 ],
 "verifier_plan": "The hidden tests will verify: 1. `naturalday` and `naturaldate` with tz-aware datetimes yield correct relative days compared to naive/system-date comparisons. 2. `_SUPERSCRIPT_MAP` is present and `scientific` produces correct output. 3. `metric` handles values like 999.9 and 999999 correctly by advancing prefixes. 4. `natural_list([])` returns '' without error."
}
